{{also|lothario}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|lothario}}

===Etymology===
Named after Lothario, a character in the play ''{{w|The Fair Penitent}}'',<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book
|year=1703
|title=The Fair Penitent
|url={{fullurl:s:en:The Fair Penitent}}
|author=Nicholas Rowe
|authorlink=Nicholas Rowe (writer)
}}</ref> a reference to a seducer of the same name in the [[metastory]] of the Quixote called ''The Impertinent Curious Man''.<ref>{{cite-book
|year=1605
|title=Don Quixote
|year_published=1885
|chapter=In which is related the novel of "The Ill-Advised Curiosity"
|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:en:Don Quixote/Volume 1/Chapter XXXIII}}
|author=Miguel de Cervantes
|authorlink=Miguel de Cervantes
|translator=John Ormsby
}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/loʊˈθɛəɹioʊ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-lothario.ogg|Audio (CA)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

#  A man whose chief [[interest]] is [[seducing]], usually women.
#: {{syn|en|ladykiller|Casanova|Romeo|Thesaurus:promiscuous man|Thesaurus:libertine}}
#: {{cot|en|Romeo}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=Schools and Schoolmasters|page=437|passage=High times, indeed, if unprincipled young rakes like him are to be permitted to invade the sanctity of domestic bliss; though do what the Bashaw will, he cannot keep the most notorious '''Lothario''' out of his bed; for alas! all fish bed in common.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1919|title=Departmental Ditties and Ballads and Barrack-Room Ballads|author=Rudyard Kipling|authorlink=Rudyard Kipling|chapter=A Code of Morals|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:en:Departmental Ditties and Ballads and Barrack-Room Ballads/A Code of Morals}}|passage=He warned her 'gainst seductive youths in scarlet clad and gold, / As much as 'gainst the blandishments paternal of the old; / But kept his gravest warnings for (hereby my ditty hangs) / That snowy-haired '''Lothario''' Lieutenant-General Bangs.}}

